      "id": 187945,
      "name": "robb8888",
      "username": "robb8888",
      "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/r/dec6dc/{size}.png",
      "created_at": "2017-01-10T23:13:01.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Powerhound_2000\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/powerhound_2000/48/1859_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Powerhound_2000:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>From Fireside chats</p>\n<div>Quote:<br><blockquote>One-shots are not considered or counted as “cards in play.” That means Into the Stratosphere or Cedistic Dissonant cannot destroy one-shots that are currently being played.\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote></div>\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Is there a difference between a card \"in play\" and one \"in a play area\"? &nbsp; Harvest the Mighty (I think) specifically uses the latter language, unlike Into the Stratosphere/Cedistic Dissonant. &nbsp; If so, then although the one-shots should not be counted to determine the number of cards \"in play\" (to see who Harvest's target hero will be), any one-shots that have not yet resolved and are still in a hero's \"play area\" should be suitable fodder for Harvest the Mighty. &nbsp;</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Sounds similar to the difference between \"playing a card\" and \"putting card into play\"; effects that prevent the former do not prevent the latter. &nbsp;</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
